**WebSockets and SSE are not handled automatically**
- Spring's graceful shutdown handles HTTP request/response cycles
- Long-lived connections need explicit close handling
- Without it, clients receive abrupt TCP disconnects and trigger aggressive reconnects

**WebSocket cleanup listener**

```java
@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class WebSocketShutdown implements ApplicationListener<ContextClosedEvent> {

    private final WebSocketSessionRegistry sessions;

    @Override
    public void onApplicationEvent(ContextClosedEvent event) {
        sessions.allActive().forEach(s -> {
            try {
                s.close(CloseStatus.GOING_AWAY);
            } catch (IOException ignore) { }
        });
    }
}
```

**SmartLifecycle for ordered teardown**
- Use when teardown order matters (consumer must stop before DB pool closes)
- Lower `getPhase()` values stop first
- More explicit than `@PreDestroy` which has no ordering guarantee across beans

```java
@Component
public class KafkaConsumerLifecycle implements SmartLifecycle {
    private volatile boolean running;
    private final KafkaConsumer<?, ?> consumer;

    @Override public void start() { running = true; }
    @Override public void stop() { consumer.wakeup(); running = false; }
    @Override public boolean isRunning() { return running; }
    @Override public int getPhase() { return 100; }
    @Override public boolean isAutoStartup() { return true; }
}
```

**Production checklist**
- `timeout-per-shutdown-phase` < `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` by at least 5s
- Readiness probe `periodSeconds` of 5-10s for fast de-registration
- `preStop` sleep of 3-5s to let kube-proxy update routing
- WebSocket and SSE explicitly closed via `ContextClosedEvent` listener
- `@Async` tasks check `Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()` for cooperative cancellation

**`@RestControllerAdvice` vs `@ControllerAdvice`**
- `@RestControllerAdvice` = `@ControllerAdvice` + `@ResponseBody`
- For REST APIs always use `@RestControllerAdvice` so handler return values serialize as JSON automatically
- Plain `@ControllerAdvice` is for MVC apps that resolve view names

**Domain-specific exception handler**

```java
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
    public ProblemDetail handleNotFound(ResourceNotFoundException ex) {
        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatus(404);
        pd.setTitle("Resource Not Found");
        pd.setDetail(ex.getMessage());
        pd.setProperty("resourceType", ex.getResourceType());
        return pd;
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(ConflictException.class)
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CONFLICT)
    public ProblemDetail handleConflict(ConflictException ex) {
        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatus(409);
        pd.setTitle("Conflict");
        pd.setDetail(ex.getMessage());
        return pd;
    }
}
```

**Handler resolution rules**
- Spring picks the most specific exception type that matches
- A handler for `IllegalArgumentException` wins over `RuntimeException`
- A handler in the controller itself takes precedence over `@ControllerAdvice` for that controller's exceptions
- This lets individual controllers override global behavior for specific cases

**Avoid the catch-all trap**
- `@ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)` returning 400 hides genuine bugs — `NullPointerException` becomes 400 instead of 500
- If you must have a catch-all, return 500 and log the full stack trace — never silently downgrade to 400

**The default behavior**
- `@Valid` on a `@RequestBody` argument triggers JSR-303 validation
- Validation failures throw `MethodArgumentNotValidException`
- Default Spring response is a 400 with Spring's own error format

**Custom handler with field-level errors**

```java
@ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)
public ProblemDetail handleValidation(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex) {
    ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatus(400);
    pd.setTitle("Validation Failed");
    pd.setDetail("One or more fields failed validation");

    Map<String, String> errors = ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors().stream()
        .collect(Collectors.toMap(
            FieldError::getField,
            FieldError::getDefaultMessage,
            (a, b) -> a + "; " + b
        ));
    pd.setProperty("fieldErrors", errors);
    return pd;
}

@ExceptionHandler(ConstraintViolationException.class)
@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)
public ProblemDetail handleConstraints(ConstraintViolationException ex) {
    ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatus(400);
    pd.setTitle("Constraint Violation");
    Map<String, String> violations = ex.getConstraintViolations().stream()
        .collect(Collectors.toMap(
            v -> v.getPropertyPath().toString(),
            ConstraintViolation::getMessage
        ));
    pd.setProperty("violations", violations);
    return pd;
}
```

**Why both handlers**
- `MethodArgumentNotValidException` — fires for `@Valid @RequestBody` validation failures
- `ConstraintViolationException` — fires for `@Validated` on path/query parameters and method arguments outside `@RequestBody`
- A complete handler covers both
